# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
import re
s = open("_recs.py", encoding="utf-8").read()
R = {
73: "Conceptual framework for interpreting sensor performance evaluations (SPEs) of fixed outdoor air-quality sensors, built on four questions: who led the evaluation, which data product was evaluated (raw, manufacturer-corrected, user-calibrated, cloud-processed), in what context (site, season, concentration range, co-location length), and what the metrics do and do not convey. It yields guiding questions that separate missing from uncertain information instead of ranking devices on headline R2 or RMSE. It does not set fitness-for-purpose thresholds and adds no co-location data, so its value depends on uptake by evaluators. Relevance: direct - this is the reporting layer every low-cost PM calibration paper needs; 'which data product' is the question most often left implicit behind published R2 values.",
34: "Dynamometer tests, chemical analysis and in vitro assays gave non-exhaust (brake and tyre wear) PM2.5 oxidative-stress and cytotoxicity potencies 5- to 11-fold higher per unit mass than exhaust PM2.5. Weighting Chinese on-road inventories by these potencies, toxicity-adjusted non-exhaust emissions in 2023 were about eight times exhaust; the non-exhaust share is projected to rise from 54% (2014) to 96% (2050) under electrification without dedicated controls. Weakness: the potency ratio comes from a small set of pads, tyres and engines under dynamometer conditions and in vitro endpoints, then is applied nationally with no reported uncertainty. Relevance: mass-based optical sensors cannot see this shift; a toxicity-weighted burden argues for composition or oxidative-potential proxies near roads.",
0: "1,898 California Teachers Study women, blood drawn 2015; residential source- and component-resolved PM0.1, PM2.5 and PM10 at 1-, 3- and 12-month lags, multinomial models per IQR, Bonferroni-corrected. PM2.5 elemental carbon was associated with top-quartile IL-1b (12-month OR 2.95, 95% CI 1.94-4.48), sCD27 (2.05, 1.34-3.13) and sTNFR2 (2.70, 1.73-4.21) at every lag; PM10 off-road diesel with IL-8 (OR 1.68, 1.23-2.30) and CCL2. PM2.5 copper and PM0.1 biomass were inversely associated with sgp130, sTNFR2 and sCD27. Weakness: one blood draw, so cross-sectional; many source x marker x lag tests; modelled component surfaces with correlated errors. Relevance: EC, the component most tractable for low-cost absorption measurement, carries the strongest immune signal.",
10: "Cascade-impactor PM in nine size fractions; ~100 components measured as ambient and lung-fluid bioaccessible concentrations, with DTT oxidative potential. Fine-particle OP was driven by elemental carbon, 9,10-phenanthrenequinone and ions, with laboratory confirmation that EC and 9,10-PQ act additively; coarse OP was dominated by bioaccessible Ni, Cu, As and Mn, ions raising OP by increasing metal bioaccessibility. PMF plus XGBoost-SHAP ranked traffic, secondary aerosol and combustion below 2.1 um, traffic and dust at 3.3-10 um. The abstract gives no sampling period, site count or effect sizes. Weakness: DTT is one acellular assay with metal-organic interferences, and SHAP importance is not source causality. Relevance: mass-only sensing misranks coarse-mode hazard; size matters beyond PM2.5.",
2: "Two-phase pilot: survey of 50 ski-industry workers (69.2% of those knowing their wax used fluorocarbon wax; 8% had effective dedicated exhaust) and field-simulated waxing and tuning with three block waxes over four stages, measured with a low-cost optical PM sensor and a PID. Wax application gave the highest run-level means, PM2.5 about 456-771 ug/m3 and PM10 602-1,045 ug/m3; brushing and scraping were intermediate; ironing raised PM only for the fluorinated race wax at 160 C; tuning was far lower. Weakness: wax type and iron temperature are confounded, the sensor was not reported as gravimetrically corrected for wax aerosol, and runs are few. Relevance: a clean case of an LCS used for stage-resolved occupational profiling, where the uncorrected mass scale is the main threat.",
7: "Concurrent belowground and street-level measurements at three Philadelphia subway stations, summer 2023. Belowground means exceeded street level about 5.1x for PM2.5, 16.5x for black carbon and 1.9x for UFP lung-deposited surface area; 15th Street was highest (PM2.5 156.8 +/- 25.3 ug/m3; LDSA 56.7 +/- 21.2 um2/cm3). A PM2.5 risk quotient below 1 was reported for commuters. Weakness: one season, three stations, no instrument models in the abstract; iron-rich subway PM makes optical mass ambient-calibrated instruments unreliable, and the low-risk conclusion rests on commuter-duration averaging and ignores workers. Relevance: subway platforms are a microenvironment where personal and low-cost monitors read high and need aerosol-specific correction.",
8: "Structured narrative review of 45 sources (PubMed/PMC, IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, Google Scholar, citation tracking) grading IoT environmental-health evidence by maturity across technical validity, data quality, interoperability, security, privacy, equity, cost and public-health utility. Evidence is strongest for field-validated PM2.5 sensing and exposure characterisation; ML calibration reduces drift when retrained across seasons or networks, but transfer is pollutant- and site-dependent; evidence that automated sensor alerts improve population health is scant. Weakness: single-author synthesis of a small, non-systematic source set with no risk-of-bias assessment, so maturity grades are judgement. Relevance: restates the gap this watch keeps finding - networks are validated as instruments, almost never as interventions with measured health or behavioural outcomes.",
55: "Intensive PM2.5 sampling in an industrial high-tech zone of the western Yangtze River Delta, November 2024-January 2025. Nitrate, sulfate and ammonium made up 62.8-91% of water-soluble ions; mean SOR 0.365, NOR 0.207; NH4+ and NO3- rose significantly from clean to polluted periods, implicating ammonium nitrate. PMF resolved seven factors; after reallocating secondary aerosol to primary sources via the local inventory, industry contributed 39.6%, mobile 14.6%, fugitive dust 11.9%, cooking 11.9%, coal 11.4%. Weakness: one site, one season, and the reallocation step imports inventory assumptions, so the industrial share is partly model-defined. Relevance: nitrate-driven episodes are where hygroscopic-growth errors in optical sensors peak, so this composition context matters for any local network.",
3: "VIIRS NOAA-20 active fires (130,766 detections after wheat and cropland masking) converted to fire-radiative-power emissions for Madhya Pradesh, 2019-2023. State CO2 ranged 111-161 Gg per year; 2022 peaked with PM2.5 0.9 Gg, OC 0.3 Gg and EC 0.2 Gg; Hoshangabad, Raisen and Vidisha were persistent hotspots; burning concentrated in March-May; HYSPLIT traced transport pathways. Weakness: FRP emission coefficients are not locally validated, small and cloud-obscured fires are missed (the authors call totals lower bounds), and there is no comparison with ground PM. Relevance: indirect - a source inventory for post-harvest burning in central India, where ground monitoring is thin; a distributed sensor network would be the missing check on satellite-derived timing and magnitude.",
6: "Ambient monitoring at residential and commercial sites in Aizawl and Lawngtlai, Mizoram, 2023-2024, aggregated to site-month means, plus a symptom survey of 261 respondents. In the February-March fire period PM10 was 74.20 vs 50.35 ug/m3 (P=0.03) and SPM 131.25 vs 84.70 ug/m3 (P=0.02); PM2.5 31.85 vs 26.69 ug/m3 was method-sensitive (Welch P=0.08, Mann-Whitney P=0.02). Breathing difficulty in the fire period: 43.82% in Aizawl vs 12.00% in Lawngtlai. Weakness: few sites, monthly aggregates and no respondent-level exposure, so the symptom contrast is between districts, not exposures. Relevance: northeast India has almost no continuous monitoring; seasonal fire PM is the signal a low-cost network would resolve daily.",
74: "Multi-sensor satellite time series (Sentinel-1/2, Landsat 8/9, MODIS, VIIRS) for the Gaza Strip, 2022-2025, quantifying land-use change, vegetation loss, surface-water extent and quality proxies, infrastructure disruption, and atmospheric impact via aerosol loading and thermal anomalies. The abstract reports increased atmospheric pollution indicators during the conflict but gives no AOD magnitude, trend or uncertainty. Weakness: column AOD over a strip of about 365 km2 is dominated by regional dust and marine aerosol, so attribution to the conflict without a control region or meteorological adjustment is not established; no surface PM is estimated. Relevance: low-moderate - where in situ monitoring is impossible, this shows the limits of AOD-only inference and the case for rapidly deployable sensors in crises.",
63: "Male Wistar rats exposed nose-only to respirable polystyrene agglomerates at 5 and 50 mg/m3 for 28 days, with about 5 and 13 weeks recovery. At 50 mg/m3: neutrophilic infiltration, type II pneumocyte and bronchiolo-alveolar hyperplasia, raised lung weight and persistent particle-associated changes - an overload profile typical of poorly soluble low-toxicity particles. At 5 mg/m3 no adverse apical effects, though lung transcripts in complement, coagulation and lipid pathways shifted; liver changes were minimal. Weakness: one polymer and particle form, male rats only, doses orders of magnitude above ambient airborne microplastic levels. Relevance: indirect - supports treating inhaled low-reactivity plastic particles within the PSLT mass-dose framework rather than as a unique hazard.",
11: "Narrative review of epidemiological, pathological and mechanistic evidence on heat and air pollution in female reproduction. Epidemiology links both to diminished ovarian reserve, lower implantation and live-birth rates, pregnancy loss, preterm birth and hypertensive disorders; pathology to impaired folliculogenesis, defective spiral-artery remodelling and endometrial receptivity; mechanisms to oxidative stress, inflammation, epigenetic reprogramming, apoptosis and autophagy. The authors name heterogeneous exposure assessment and poorly defined susceptible windows as the main limits. Weakness: no search strategy or quantitative synthesis, and heat and PM evidence are merged, so no PM-specific dose-response can be extracted. Relevance: low - but the stated bottleneck, exposure in narrow windows such as peri-implantation, is what personal or residential sensing addresses.",
35: "Review reframing dust storms as dynamic multipollutant exposure systems: mineral particles acquire anthropogenic and secondary components during transport, so toxicity reflects coatings, metals and biological content rather than crustal mass alone. It integrates environmental chemistry, exposure science, toxicology and epidemiology to propose a basis for dust-specific health risk assessment. The abstract gives no search method, quantitative synthesis or effect estimates. Weakness: conceptual; the claim that transport-aged dust is more toxic per unit mass is plausible but not quantified here. Relevance: moderate - optical low-cost sensors undercount coarse dust and mass alone misses ageing, so dust events are where calibration and toxicity weighting fail together.",
75: "Review of 27 studies across North America, Europe, Asia and Oceania on aerosolised harmful algal and cyanobacterial bloom toxins. Fourteen toxins were detected in aerosol; microcystin appeared in 41% of studies (marine and freshwater, maximum 40.0 ng/m3), brevetoxin in 37% (all marine), BMAA in 11%. Sampling (filters, cascade impactors, impingers) and analysis (ELISA, LC-MS/MS, LC-HRMS) differ widely; transport of kilometres to hundreds of kilometres inland is reported. Weakness: few studies with incomparable methods, no exposure-response data, and size distributions of toxin-bearing aerosol rarely reported. Relevance: a bioaerosol pathway invisible to mass-based PM monitoring; size-resolved sampling near bloom waters is the missing measurement layer.",
}
for k, v in R.items():
    s, n = re.subn(r'(add\(%d, sub=(?:(?!\nadd\().)*?\n    n=)"X"\)' % k, lambda m: m.group(1) + '"' + v + '")', s, count=1, flags=re.S)
    assert n == 1, k
open("_recs.py", "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)
for m in re.finditer(r'add\((\d+),(?:(?!\nadd\().)*?\n    n="(.*?)"\)', s, re.S):
    print(m.group(1), len(m.group(2).split()), end="; ")
